'''affuse''' (a-fūz′), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''affusus'', pp. of ''affundere'', ''adfundere'', pour upon, < ''ad'', to, + ''fundere'', pour: see ''fuse''<sup>1</sup>.] To pour. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|I first ''affused'' water upon the compressed beans.
{{right|''Boyle'', Works, IV. 568.}}}}

'''affusion''' (a-fū′zhọn), ''n.'' [< ML. ''affusio(n-)'', < L. ''affundere'', pour upon: see ''affuse''.] '''1.''' The act of pouring upon; the act of pouring water or other liquid, as upon a child in baptism.
{{smaller block|When the Jews baptized their children, in order to circumcision, it seems to have been indifferent whether it was done by immersion or ''affusion''.
{{right|''Wheatly'', Ill. of Book of Com. Prayer, p. 362.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''med.'', the act of pouring water on the body as a curative means, as from a vessel, by a shower-bath, etc.
{{smaller block|When I travell’d in Italy, and the Southern parts, I did sometimes frequent the publiq bathes, . . . but seldome without peril of my life ’till I us’d this frigid ''affusion'', or rather profusion of cold water before I put on my garments.
{{right|''Evelyn'', To Doctor Beale.}}
Some of these [remedies] are ''affusion'', half-baths, . . .}}

